      "writtenOn": "2022-07-25T22:18:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hmmm, yes a matrix wrapper + (path | drawable | bitmap) is a general thing we will want to do. At the moment we do need a drawable per size because we don\u0027t currently have a \"generic strike\" concept, though we\u0027re eventually going to need something like it. It isn\u0027t possible in the general case since glyph paths and even the paths inside the drawable may be size specific (hinted). Every implementation at the moment needs a drawable wrapper for some reason anyway.\n\n1) In general one doesn\u0027t want to call any of the glyph setters in a way that will take up space. Setting the drawable in generateMetrics will set the drawable, but the strike won\u0027t know how much space it may take up and won\u0027t include it in the cache size. It\u0027s fine to set no-paths on the glyph because they don\u0027t really take up any additional space.\n\n2) I\u0027m not entirely sure what you mean here. In the image path the glyph bounds are calculated based on what will be drawn. The only thing the glyph bounds state is that the glyph won\u0027t draw outside those bounds. The glyph image is \"implicitly clipped\" to the glyph bounds but the idea is this never actually clips anything (unless potentially the cached information in the font is incorrect). In the same way we clip to the drawable bounds because that\u0027s what the drawable said its bounds were, so we clip to that to ensure it doesn\u0027t draw outside that area. The glyph bounds generated should always match what will be used to draw (and may not reflect other possible ways the glyph may be drawn if the user were to get the path or drawable or whatnot directly). But it\u0027s fine to let the bounds be a bit bigger (round them out) since the only thing they indicate is that the drawing won\u0027t go outside that.\n\nYou don\u0027t need to add it to this CL directly (though I\u0027d probably end up adding it anyway at some point) the following makes it easier to see the bounds of things and how things were drawn (of course set `kSkShowTextBlitCoverage` to `true` to actually enable). This is based on code that all the other `SkScalerContext` subclasses use.\n\n```\n+namespace {\n+static inline const constexpr bool kSkShowTextBlitCoverage \u003d false;\n+}\n+\n static SkFontMetrics scale_fontmetrics(const SkFontMetrics\u0026 src, float sx, float sy) {\n     SkFontMetrics dst \u003d src;\n \n@@ -281,7 +285,11 @@ protected:\n         auto canvas \u003d SkCanvas::MakeRasterDirectN32(glyph.fWidth, glyph.fHeight,\n                                                     static_cast\u003cSkPMColor*\u003e(glyph.fImage),\n                                                     glyph.rowBytes());\n-        canvas-\u003eclear(SK_ColorTRANSPARENT);\n+        if constexpr (kSkShowTextBlitCoverage) {\n+            canvas-\u003eclear(0x33FF0000);\n+        } else {\n+            canvas-\u003eclear(SK_ColorTRANSPARENT);\n+        }\n         canvas-\u003etranslate(-glyph.fLeft, -glyph.fTop);\n         canvas-\u003etranslate(SkFixedToScalar(glyph.getSubXFixed()),\n                           SkFixedToScalar(glyph.getSubYFixed()));\n@@ -317,6 +325,12 @@ protected:\n             }\n \n             void onDraw(SkCanvas* canvas) override {\n+                if constexpr (kSkShowTextBlitCoverage) {\n+                    SkPaint paint;\n+                    paint.setColor(0x3300FF00);\n+                    paint.setStyle(SkPaint::kFill_Style);\n+                    canvas-\u003edrawRect(this-\u003eonGetBounds(), paint);\n+                }\n                 canvas-\u003edrawDrawable(fDrawable.get(), \u0026fMatrix);\n             }\n         private:\n         ```",
